  "sizing": {
    "description": "How big every visual preview in a chat reply is (Markdown and inline images, image file references, SVG, a PDF's pages, a video's frame), shared by the desktop (crates/ui/src/markdown/preview_size.rs) and iOS; the same section is in image-sources-v1.json and file-previews-v1.json. Not covered: tool-row thumbnails, csv/tsv tables and the html page card (a full-width row). All lengths are logical points (no pixel scale); expected values are exact up to 4 decimals: compare within 0.01 (a platform may snap to its pixel grid when drawing). `previewSize(column, natural)`: `column` is the reply column's width, `natural` the content's natural size (only its aspect matters). The preview is always `column` wide (a small image or an SVG is upscaled). `contentHeight` = column * natural.height / natural.width, the height the whole content draws at. When contentHeight <= column * caps.crop.height / caps.crop.width (landscape, square, up to exactly 4:5) the preview shows it whole: height = contentHeight, cropped = false. Taller: height = column * crop.height / crop.width and cropped = true: the content still draws column wide and contentHeight tall, its top edge at the preview's top (horizontally it fills the width, so it is centred), and the part below the preview is cut off; a click still opens the whole thing. A natural or column size that is not positive shows nothing (all zero). PDF: its pages stack at full width, each `column` wide and `contentHeight` of its own size tall, never cropped. `pdf` cases: `pdfPages(pages)` -> `shown` = min(pages, caps.pdfPages) pages, `more` = the rest, and when more > 0 a row below them reading `label` (\"1 more page\", else \"N more pages\") that opens the file; `label` is null when more = 0.",
    "caps": {
      "crop": {
        "width": 4,
